Spec: Git Client

Purpose:
  Read regular files at one resolved commit from a remote Git repository through an isolated temporary repository.

Owns:
  ./git-client.ts
  ./git-client.test.ts

References:
  ./file-system.sdd
  ./temp-directory.sdd
  ../cli-error.sdd
  ../git-ref.sdd

Must:
  Accept @FileSystem, @TempDirectory, and an injectable process runner through constructor parameters.
  Accept a repository transport URL, a repository-relative file path, and an optional exact tag, branch, or commit identifier reachable from a branch or tag.
  Validate supplied ref spelling through @isValidGitRef before invoking Git.
  Use the remote default branch when no ref is supplied.
  Resolve the selected ref to a full commit identifier, including dereferencing annotated tags.
  Report ambiguous refs and unavailable commits as errors rather than silently choosing another ref.
  Fetch only branch and tag refs and their complete reachable history; other ref namespaces, including pull-request refs, are not fetched.
  Fetch into a unique temporary repository without a working-tree checkout and read the file's exact blob bytes at the resolved commit.
  Require every present tree entry to be a regular file; reject symlinks, directories, submodules, and missing required entries.
  @GitClient.readFiles reads required and optional paths in one clone at the same resolved commit; only an absent optional tree entry is omitted from the result.
  Invoke Git with an argument vector and shell execution disabled, separating user-supplied arguments from command options.
  Restrict remote transports to HTTPS and SSH and preserve Git's normal credential and SSH authentication mechanisms.
  Delegate SSH authentication to Git and the caller's SSH client using the existing agent, preserving `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, SSH configuration including `IdentityAgent` and host aliases, and explicit `GIT_SSH` or `GIT_SSH_COMMAND` settings.
  Preserve the caller's SSH host-key verification configuration.
  Remove the temporary repository after successful reads and failed operations; report cleanup failure without hiding the original error.
  Report a missing Git executable, authentication failure, unavailable repository, unresolved ref, and unreadable file through @GitClientError.
  Exclude embedded authentication secrets from errors and diagnostics.

Must not:
  Check out repository files, initialize submodules, or run repository hooks and downloaded scripts.
  Start a separate SSH agent, load or copy private keys, or manage SSH credentials directly.
  Retry a failed SSH operation over HTTPS automatically.

Depends on:
  Git executable
  SSH client and the caller's existing authentication agent for SSH transport
  Node.js child process support
  @FileSystem
  @TempDirectory

Exposes:
  @GitClient
  @GitClient.readFile
  @GitClient.readFiles

Returns:
  @GitFileResult containing the full resolved commit identifier and exact file bytes
  @GitFilesResult containing the full resolved commit identifier and a map of requested paths to exact blob bytes

Raises:
  @GitClientError extending @CliError

Scenario: existing SSH agent
  Given the caller has an authenticated SSH agent available through `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`
  When @GitClient.readFile accesses an SSH repository
  Then the Git process inherits that agent socket and the caller's SSH configuration
  And authentication is delegated to Git and SSH without separate key handling

Scenario: SSH authentication failure
  Given the caller's SSH authentication cannot access the repository
  When @GitClient.readFile accesses that repository
  Then a @GitClientError reports the SSH authentication failure
  And no HTTPS retry is attempted

Scenario: pinned historical commit
  Given a requested commit is reachable from a repository branch or tag
  When @GitClient.readFile reads a file at that commit
  Then it obtains that commit and returns the file bytes from that commit

Scenario: commit reachable only through a pull-request ref
  Given a commit is reachable only through `refs/pull/1/head` and not through any branch or tag
  When that commit identifier or pull-request ref is requested
  Then a @GitClientError reports that the requested ref is unavailable

Scenario: symbolic link source
  Given the requested file is a symbolic link in the selected commit
  When @GitClient.readFile reads that path
  Then a @GitClientError is raised
  And the link target is not read
